# PreLife — production env template (for Render).
#
# ARCHITECTURE (v5.0): the backend is the RUST SERVICE
# (mini-services/prelife-api). Deploy it next to the Next.js server and
# route /api/* to it at the edge (Render: a second service + rewrite, or
# Caddy/nginx: handle /api/* -> reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3030). The vars
# below feed BOTH processes.
#
# Two ways to feed these to the Render deployment:
#
#   1. PREFERRED: Render dashboard → PreLife service → Environment →
#      add SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY as variables.
#      (Values set there are injected straight into the process env.)
#
#   2. Or copy this file to .env.production (git-ignored!) and fill in the
#      real values — `npm start` loads it via `node --env-file-if-exists`
#      and `next build` reads it at build time (NEXT_PUBLIC_* inlining).
#      Only do this if the repository is PRIVATE.
#
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://prelife.onrender.com
# ── Outgoing e-mail (Brevo HTTP API — the only transport; mail.rs) ─────────
# Add BREVO_API_KEY in the Render dashboard → Environment (same place as
# the Supabase vars). The key is the Brevo v3 API key (xkeysib-…);
# the sender no-reply@prelife.net is already domain-verified.
# Free plan = 300 mails/day.
BREVO_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=PreLife <no-reply@prelife.net>

# ── AI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-flash-lite-latest

# ── The Rust backend ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Build with `cargo build --release` (see mini-services/prelife-api/README).
# NODE_ENV=production disables the emulator/dev links; leave
# API_TRANSFORM_PORT / NEXT_PUBLIC_API_TRANSFORM_PORT unset so the
# frontend calls plain same-origin /api/* paths.
# PUBLIC_URL: canonical origin — the backend's origin allowlist for
# verification links, OAuth redirects and cookie Secure decisions.
PUBLIC_URL=https://prelife.onrender.com
#
# API_INTERNAL_URL — where the NEXT SERVER's SSR data layer (lib/
# server-api.ts) finds the Rust service. Same host: leave the default
# (http://127.0.0.1:3030). Rust service deployed as a SEPARATE host: set
# it to that internal URL, or every server-rendered content page silently
# falls back to the "content unavailable" shell.
#API_INTERNAL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3030
